<Layout
	title="About"
	description=""
	pubDate={new Date('September 13, 2026')}
>
	<p>
		For a few decades after World War II, the United States
		built something close to broad-based prosperity. 
		Median wealth grew alongside the economy. That trend 
		reversed. Markets and major asset classes have 
		increasingly decoupled from the financial position of 
		the typical American, concentrating gains at the top 
		while housing costs outpace wages nearly everywhere.
	</p>

	<p>
		Housing sits at the center of this because it has 
		stopped functioning primarily as shelter and started 
		functioning as an investment vehicle. That shift has 
		consequences: rising homelessness in the country's 
		wealthiest metros, and neighborhoods once home to 
		working families being rebuilt for high earners who 
		can work from anywhere.
	</p>

	<p>
		The same forces are reshaping the map. Young people 
		leave rural towns for college and never come back, 
		chasing jobs concentrated in a handful of large metros. 
		That migration is hollowing out rural America's 
		economic base while flooding metro job markets, pushing 
		wages down and housing costs up in the same places 
		already straining under demand. Rural decline and urban 
		affordability may look like separate problems, but 
		they're two sides of the same imbalance.

	<p>
		We don't treat housing as a right. We treat it, like 
		food and water, as a basic human need, and we think a 
		society's treatment of that need is worth measuring 
		carefully. 32:18 exists to quantify this divide with 
		data rather than argument, and to publish research 
		that lets the numbers make the case.
	</p>

</Layout>
